<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Preslav</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/893">893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> was an assembly of Bulgarian nobles and clergy held at Preslav in 893. Summoned by the former ruler Boris I, it confirmed the deposition of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-rasate">Vladimir-Rasate</a>, who had tried to restore paganism, and placed his younger son Simeon I on the throne. It is traditionally said also to have made Slavonic the language of church and state and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> to Preslav.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Boris I abdicated in 889 and retired to a monastery, leaving the throne to his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-rasate">Vladimir-Rasate</a>, who tried to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrism</a>, the country's traditional religion since 681. In 893 Boris deposed him and called a People's Council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> to give the change legitimacy, with the higher and lower nobility, the clergy and representatives of every province.</p><p>No medieval source mentions the council directly. The fullest account of Vladimir's fall comes from the Benedictine abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regino-of-prum">Regino of Prüm</a>, whose Chronicon tells how Boris left the monastery, captured his son, had his eyes put out and imprisoned him, then gathered his whole realm, set up his younger son as prince and threatened him with the same punishment should he betray <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Historians hold that the council, presided over by Boris, took four main decisions. Simeon I, who had been meant for a high church career, was released from his monastic vows and replaced his brother, and the rule of succession was changed so that a ruler's brother could follow him. The capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, where memories of the pagan past were strong, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>, home of the Panteleimon Monastery where Boris had retired.</p><p>Byzantine clergy were to be sent away and replaced by Bulgarian clerics, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a>, sent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> to Devol. Old Bulgarian was to replace Greek in the liturgy, becoming the official language of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The official status of Old Bulgarian gave impetus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and Ohrid Literary Schools, and under Simeon Bulgarian culture and literature entered a golden age, making the country a cultural centre of Slavic Europe. Removing the Byzantine clergy and Greek liturgy kept Bulgarian religious life and policy free of direct Byzantine influence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> answered in 894, when Emperor Leo VI moved the Bulgarian merchants' market from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a>, a move that led to war, won by Simeon at the Battle of Boulgarophygon in 896.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Preslav” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
